    "content": "During a stop at Andrews Air Force Base, President Donald Trump was asked by a reporter about the possibility of seeking a third term in office. He responded that he would be open to continuing his leadership, citing public support, but conceded that the U.S. Constitution imposes a firm restriction.\n\nThe relevant provision is the 22nd Amendment, ratified in 1951, which states that no person can be elected to the presidency more than twice. This rule was introduced following Franklin D. Roosevelt's four terms in office, reflecting a national consensus to prevent prolonged concentration of executive power.\n\nTrump's remarks come amid ongoing debates about presidential term limits in American politics. While some supporters have floated constitutional amendments or legal interpretations, any change would require a supermajority in Congress and ratification by three-fourths of states, making it a remote possibility.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this news underscores the stability of U.S. democratic institutions, which is a key factor in diaspora engagement and investment. Many Somali Americans closely follow U.S. political developments, as policies on immigration, trade, and international aid directly affect their lives and connections to Somalia.\n\nThe discussion also highlights the contrast with political systems in some East African nations, where term limits have been contested. Understanding these differences helps diasporic communities navigate civic participation in their host countries.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/13/trump-sees-law-as-obstacle-to-third-term/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=trump-sees-law-as-obstacle-to-third-term",
